          "summary": "BIS research identifies a shifting systemic risk landscape as non-bank financial institutions (NBFIs) increasingly integrate into the sovereign-bank nexus.",
          "whyItMatters": "The expansion of the sovereign-bank-NBFI nexus complicates financial stability oversight as risks now propagate through less regulated non-bank channels. Investors must account for these interconnected vulnerabilities when assessing sovereign debt sustainability and liquidity risks in stressed market conditions.",
          "keyNumbers": [],
          "marketImpact": "Increased NBFI participation in sovereign bond markets may heighten volatility during liquidity crunches, potentially forcing fire sales that impact broader fixed-income pricing. Portfolios heavily weighted in sovereign debt should account for the increased counterparty and systemic risk posed by NBFI leverage.",
          "indiaImpact": "As India's NBFI sector grows and deepens its holdings in government securities, the Reserve Bank of India faces increased complexity in monitoring systemic contagion risks. This highlights the need for robust macroprudential frameworks to manage the evolving interdependencies between banks, mutual funds, and sovereign debt.",
          "globalImpact": "The transition from a bank-centric to a broader financial nexus suggests that global financial stability is increasingly dependent on the resilience of the shadow banking sector. This shift necessitates a more holistic regulatory approach to monitor sovereign debt transmission mechanisms across borders.",
